@page "/testing"

<DocPage Href="testing" Title="Testing"
         Lead="Stores are plain objects, so they test like plain objects - and Bit.Blex.Testing adds a harness for asserting on the dispatch pipeline itself.">

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The simplest test</h3>
        <p>
            A store needs no host, no DI container and no Blazor. Construct it, call an action, assert on
            a property.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Plain" FileName="CounterTests.cs" />

        <p>
            That covers state. What it does not cover is whether the <em>right action</em> was dispatched,
            with the right payload, exactly once. That is what the harness is for.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> The harness</h3>
        <p>
            <code>BlexTestHarness&lt;TStore&gt;</code> creates a fresh <code>BlexManager</code>,
            registers your store with it, and starts recording every action.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Harness" />
        <ApiTable Title="BlexTestHarness&lt;TStore&gt;" Rows="@HarnessRows" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> The action log</h3>
        <p>
            <code>BlexActionLog</code> records each dispatch with its name, sequence, arguments and the global
            state immediately afterwards.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Log" />
        <ApiTable Title="BlexActionLog" Rows="@LogRows" />
        <ApiTable Title="BlexRecordedAction" Rows="@RecordedRows" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Asserting on payloads</h3>
        <p>Action arguments are captured automatically, so you can assert on exactly what was dispatched.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Payload" />

        <p>
            Indexing <code>Args</code> by position and casting <code>Value</code> works, but it breaks
            silently - or with an <code>InvalidCastException</code> nobody can read - the day the action
            changes shape. The log also answers in terms of the generated
            <a href="actions#typed-actions">descriptors</a>, handing back each payload as the type the action
            takes:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@TypedPayload" Title="Typed" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="time">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Deterministic time</h3>
        <p>
            A <code>DebounceMilliseconds</code> effect waits for real time to pass, and so does a debounced
            persistor. A test that waits along with them is slow, and on a loaded CI runner it is flaky.
            Everything Blex times - action timestamps, the debounce of generated effects, the persistence
            debounce and its cap - reads the manager's <code>TimeProvider</code>, so a test swaps in a fake
            clock and <em>advances</em> it instead of sleeping.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Time" />
        <ul class="bb-detail-list">
            <li><code>FakeTimeProvider</code> comes from <code>Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing</code>; any <code>TimeProvider</code> works.</li>
            <li>Set the clock before the work it should govern starts: a wait already in progress keeps the clock it began on.</li>
            <li>In a DI host, <code>options.TimeProvider</code> sets it; when unset, a <code>TimeProvider</code> registered in the container is used, and <code>TimeProvider.System</code> otherwise.</li>
            <li>A store reads the same clock through its protected <code>TimeProvider</code> - stamp an action with <code>TimeProvider.GetUtcNow()</code> and the test controls that too.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="replay">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Replaying actions</h3>
        <p>
            The defining property of a predictable state model is that the same actions, applied to the same
            initial state, arrive at the same state - whichever session produced them. Because every action
            is <a href="actions#dispatch-by-name">dispatchable by name</a>, a recorded log can be replayed
            onto a fresh store and the two compared. It is also how a bug report becomes a test: record the
            actions that led to it, then replay them.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Replay" />
        <p>
            Each action runs through its generated wrapper again, so filters, middleware and effects behave
            exactly as they did the first time. Generated actions and effects, <code>Set X</code>
            assignments and <code>ResetState</code> replay; a <code>Batch</code> carries a closure rather
            than data, so it cannot, and the replay stops with an <code>InvalidOperationException</code>
            naming the first action it could not dispatch. The list is copied first, so replaying into the
            manager that is recording it is safe.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Testing effects</h3>
        <p>
            Awaiting the effect wrapper is usually enough. When you need to observe an intermediate state
            - a loading flag, say - <code>WaitForAsync</code> beats sprinkling <code>Task.Delay</code>
            through the test: it re-evaluates the condition on every store change and throws
            <code>TimeoutException</code> if it never becomes true.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Effects" />

        <h4>Errors and cancellation</h4>
        <CodeBlock Code="@EffectErrors" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Asserting on batching</h3>
        <p>
            <code>CountNotifications</code> counts how many times a store raised
            <code>StateChanged</code> while an action ran - the direct way to prove that a multi-field
            action really does render once.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Notifications" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Testing middleware</h3>
        <p>
            A store is rarely alone in production - a veto filter, an audit logger or a metrics recorder
            usually wraps it, and those guard rails deserve tests too.
            <code>BlexTestHarness.WithFilter</code> and <code>WithMiddleware</code> give you the same
            zero-setup harness with the pipeline attached.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@MiddlewareTest" />

        <p>
            Reach for a hand-built <code>BlexManager</code> only when the test needs a manager configured
            before any store is registered.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> More than one store</h3>
        <p>
            Cross-store coordination is exactly the kind of thing worth testing, and abandoning the
            harness to hand-wire a manager for it defeats the point. <code>harness.Register(store)</code>
            brings a second store onto the same manager: its actions land in the same log and its slice
            in the same <code>State</code> tree, and it is disposed with the harness.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@MultiStore" />

        <p>
            For a condition that spans stores - or one the store you are driving will not report -
            <code>manager.WaitForAsync</code> re-checks after every action <em>and</em> every restore in
            the application, where <code>store.WaitForAsync</code> only watches one store.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Asserting on failures</h3>
        <p>
            An effect captures its exception into <code>XxxError</code> instead of propagating it, so a
            test that only watches for a throw sees nothing at all. <code>harness.Failures</code> records
            every action whose body threw, with the arguments it was called with.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@FailureTest" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Testing persistence</h3>
        <p>
            <code>IBlexStorage</code> is a three-method interface, so an in-memory fake is trivial.
            Remember to <code>FlushAsync</code> when the persistor is debounced.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@PersistenceTest" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Testing undo/redo</h3>
        <CodeBlock Code="@HistoryTest" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Extension methods</h3>
        <ApiTable Title="BlexTestExtensions" Rows="@ExtensionRows" />

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Tip" Title="Snapshots make good assertions">
            <p>
                <code>harness.Snapshot()</code> and <code>harness.State</code> return the same JSON the
                library uses for persistence and time-travel. Asserting against a snapshot checks the
                whole store at once, and it fails loudly when a field you forgot about changes.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Gotcha" Title="Dispose the harness">
            <p>
                The harness holds a live subscription on its manager. Use <code>using var</code> - or
                <code>Dispose()</code> - so a long test class does not accumulate recorders. The
                harness's manager is its own, so tests never interfere with each other.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Testing components</h3>
        <p>
            For component-level tests with bUnit, register the store the same way the app does and let
            the component subscribe. There is nothing Blex-specific to mock.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Bunit" />
    </div>

</DocPage>

